TEST 38478 -- ATMF - remote login
===========================================================================
Run:    2026-09-21, tb470
DUT:    IE520 4-member VCStack, AMF node "IE520-stk"
Peer:   AR4050S-5G, AMF node "4050-5g", AMF master
AMF:    network-name "tb470", 2 nodes, AMF link po1

VERDICT: PASS (both directions)

STEP -- "Check that when the DUT is part of an AMF network that remote login
         works to and from the DUT"

  A) master -> DUT :  atmf remote-login IE520-stk
     landed = YES, returned home = YES
     POSITIVE PROOF (show system on the remote) returned FOUR AT-IE520-28GSX
     boards, S/N 264A23061 / 264A23068 / 264A23066 / 264A23052 -- i.e. the
     session really was on the IE520 stack, not a local echo.
     Landed at a USER EXEC prompt: IE520-stk>

  B) DUT -> master :  atmf remote-login 4050-5g
     landed = YES, returned home = YES
     POSITIVE PROOF returned Base 598 AR4050S-5G S/N A10401G214000005 -- the
     session really was on the AR4050S.
     Landed at a USER EXEC prompt: 4050-5g>

  Both sessions exited cleanly back to their own node (verified by re-reading
  "show atmf | include Node Name" after exit).

OBSERVATION (not a failure, recorded for the next session): BOTH directions
land in USER EXEC (>), not privileged EXEC, even though the originating session
was privileged (#) on its own node. The remote session announces itself with
"Type 'exit' to return to <origin>." and drops privilege. The case does not
state an expected privilege level, so this is logged rather than graded -- but
anyone scripting against remote-login must expect ">" and enable if they need
privileged commands.

OBSERVATION: "Restricted login" read "Enabled" before the AMF network was
configured and "Disabled" on both nodes afterwards. Restricted login being off
is why direction B was permitted; with it on, only the master may remote-login.

